FWIW, Crawl resolves the dilemma your post, in the context of the
thread, indirectly points to -- some players vehemently love grinding
and the possibilities opened up by it, and some vehemently hate
gameplay balanced to account for the option of grinding -- by providing
a grinder race (Mummies: you don't eat, so you can sit around forever),
a semi-grindish god (Nemelex: collect and sacrifice random junk to keep
him happy; as long as he's sufficiently happy, he will provide at
random intervals items with effects that, though some are
hyperdangerous, include statgains with a VERY high cap), and, for *all*
PC types, not one but two grinding-oriented (infinite) optional regions
(Abyss and Pandemonium), both so high in difficulty and (unless you're
*un*lucky) so late in the game that they're not so much places you go
to prepare for finite regions as places that you'd better have already
gotten prepared for in the finite regions.

When I first started playing Crawl, I was quite puzzled by the
existence of these three elements, especially the first two, as they
fly in the face of everything else about the game. I'm beginning to
understand their existence more over time -- Linley (or the devteam
that picked up from him, if they came from that team) was more a Linus
than a Richard. :-)

And indeed, Crawl does offer extinctionist-like challenges doable using
one or more of the mentioned elements, such as the already-mentioned
all-rune run (with five of those runes being available after a random
amount of grinding ranging from half an hour to half a week), or an
all-skills-maxed game.

If any of this provides inspirations for tweaks to Nethack that are
still in its spirit but help it better accomodate both grind-lovers and
hard-but-not-boring lovers, I'll be tickled pink!

